    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda CRF1150L Africa Twin Enduro Sports Is a Tongue Twister, Weighs Less but Offers More\nMan Pulls Off Heist, Buys 2019 Chevrolet Corvette ZR1 Coupe 3ZR ZTK for $158,000\n19 photos\nPhoto: Bring a Trailer\nThe market for used C7 Corvette ZR1s is fascinating. Manual examples are fetching ridiculous sums of money, sometimes selling for nearly 2x their MSRP. Meanwhile, the automatic versions are considerably more affordable, which of course makes sense to a certain extent. That being said, this Arctic White example with \"almost delivery miles\" selling for $158,000 at auction was an absolute steal. I'm a little speechless.\nBack in May, we saw a pretty much identical model, albeit with the 7-speed manual gearbox, sell for $275,000 - and it had 1,600 miles on the clock. Then, in June, another 2019 ZR1 7-speed went for $242,000 with just under 8,000 miles on its supercharged LT5 V8. I get how collectible this car is with a manual transmission, but to go from that... to $158,000 on a car that's only covered 201 miles is absolutely incredible.\nSo yes, this thing was stolen like it just left the set of an Ocean's Eleven movie. This thing is more \"stolen\" than the Declaration of Independence in Nicolas Cage's National Treasure movie. This thing is... okay, alright, I think you got the point.\nIn terms of specs, you've got everything you need on this car, which is finished in Arctic White with an Adrenaline Red leather and black microsuede interior. Personally, I like the contrast, especially with the exposed carbon fiber elements and Carbon Flash 19\" front and 20\" rear wheels. It's a gorgeous spec, although surprisingly, I'd probably take Torch Red over Arctic White. At least on the C7 Corvette. On the C8, it would be the other way around.\nThe ZTK package is responsible for that massive adjustable rear wing, but also the front splitter end caps, stiffer springs, and the revised Magnetic Ride Control tuning. The wing is actually bolted directly to the chassis, which immediately tells you just how serious of a car this is.\nOther exterior highlights include the removable carbon fiber roof panel, vented carbon fiber hood, xenon headlights, quad exhaust outlets, plus carbon ceramic brakes with red Brembo calipers.\nMoving along to the interior, that's where we'll find the heated and ventilated GT bucket seats, carbon fiber trim, a Bose sound system, sat-nav, dual-zone automatic climate control, a head-up display, the carbon fiber and microsuede steering wheel, and more.\nPhoto: Bring a Trailer As for performance, the supercharged 6.2-liter LT5 V8 is pushing 755 horsepower and 715 lb-ft of torque to the rear wheels via an eight-speed automatic transmission and an electronic limited-slip differential. According to GM, the C7 ZR1 can hit 60 mph (97 kph) in 2.85 seconds, but in reality, think more like 3 seconds flat. And that's with the automatic transmission. The 7-speed manual variant should be a tiny bit slower, obviously.\nUltimately, this is an incredible purchase for $158k. And I understand the appeal of manual transmissions on supercars as well as the next guy, but there's no way I'd spend $80k+ on the same car just to shift gears myself.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChevrolet Corvette ZR1 Corvette ZR1 Chevrolet Chevrolet Corvette Corvette ZR1 used cars auction\nAbout the author: Sergiu Tudose\nSergiu got to experience both American and European car \"scenes\" at an early age (his father drove a Ford Fiesta XR2 supermini in the 80s). After spending over 15 years at local and international auto publications, he's starting to appreciate comfort behind the wheel more than raw power and acceleration. Full profile\nUP NEXT Driven: 2026 Subaru Trailseeker (e-Outback) Proves the Boxer Engine Isn't Everything",
